#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166644 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "280d5568-b89a-5f92-97b1-a0489f139705" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square to Compute a Hyperbolic Arsecant Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "f78d6af8-1a67-50e5-a1d0-6afbbe2b7c16" :question/math-academy-id "e-8452" :question/problem "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)\\sqrt{2x-x^2}} \\textrm{d}x=}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x-x^2 & = -(x^2 - 2x) \\\\[5pt] & = -(x^2 - 2\\cdot x + 1^2-1^2) \\\\[5pt] & = -((x-1)^2 - 1) \\\\[5pt] &= 1 - (x-1)^2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)\\sqrt{2x-x^2}} \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)\\sqrt {1-(x-1)^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x-1$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int(1)/((x - 1)\\sqrt{2x - x^{2}})dx &= \\int(1)/((x - 1)\\sqrt{1 - (x - 1)^{2}})dx \\\\\n&= \\int\\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}du \\\\\n&=-arsech u + C \\\\\n&=-arsech (x - 1) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166645 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a67a245-b5be-5fe5-b6e4-c4a5f4bee054" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Inverse Reciprocal Hyperbolic Integral Containing a Completed Square"}] :question/id #uuid "cde0faf2-8650-59c7-9a28-073516b09594" :question/math-academy-id "e-8453" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{4-(2x-2)^2} \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{4-(2x-2)^2}\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{1}{4-4(x-1)^2}\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{4} {\\int}\\dfrac{1}{1 - (x-1)^2}\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x-1$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{4}\\int(1)/(1 - (x - 1)^{2})dx &= \\frac{1}{4}\\int\\frac{1}{1 - u^{2}} \\cdot du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot arcoth u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}arcoth (x - 1) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition\n\n$$\n\\left|{x-1} \\right| > 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166646 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f97720d6-2c67-5017-bf20-8045a8bb30b5" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square to Compute a Hyperbolic Arcosecant Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "26cd55d1-dc94-507c-bcf9-13d619493184" :question/math-academy-id "e-8454" :question/problem "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{6}{\\mid 3x+9 \\mid \\sqrt{x^2+6x+10}} \\textrm{d}x=}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 6x + 10 &= x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3x + 3^{2} - 3^{2} + 10 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{6}{\\begin{vmatrix}3x\\!+\\!9 & \\sqrt{x^2\\!+\\!6x\\!+\\!10}} \\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{6}{3\\begin{vmatrix}x\\!+\\!3 & \\sqrt {1\\!+\\!(x\\!+\\!3)^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x = {2} \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\end{vmatrix}x\\!+\\!3\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{1\\!+\\!(x\\!+\\!3)^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nu = x+3\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\int(1)/(\\begin{vmatrix}x + 3 & \\sqrt{1 + (x + 3)^{2}})dx &= 2\\int\\frac{1}{\\mid u\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{1 + u^{2}}}du \\\\\n&=-2arcsch u + C \\\\\n&=-2arcsch (x + 3) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166647 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6640d03e-5194-5a31-9315-007e59732446" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square to Compute a Hyperbolic Arcotangent Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "aa11660f-ce84-52df-bcc4-45a6d63b6d7e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8455" :question/problem "$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{3-2x-x^2} \\textrm{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3-2x-x^2 & = -(x^2 + 2x - 3) \\\\[5pt] & = -(x^2 + 2\\cdot x +1^2 - 1^2 - 3) \\\\[5pt] & = -((x+1)^2 - 4) \\\\[5pt] &= 4 - (x+1)^2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator of our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{3-2x-x^2}\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{1}{4-(x+1)^2}\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{4} {\\int}\\dfrac{1}{1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}(x+1)\\right)^2}\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac{1}{2}(x+1)\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{4}\\int(1)/(1 - (\\frac{1}{2}(x + 1))^{2})dx &= \\frac{1}{4}\\int\\frac{1}{1 - u^{2}} \\cdot 2du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int\\frac{1}{1 - u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot arcoth u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot arcoth (\\frac{1}{2}(x + 1)) + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}arcoth (\\frac{x + 1}{2}) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac {x+1} {2} \\right| > 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a67a245-b5be-5fe5-b6e4-c4a5f4bee054" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Inverse Reciprocal Hyperbolic Integral Containing a Completed Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb62d6dc-b057-5891-a51d-b84340e97c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}arsech (x - 1) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165807} {:answer/id #uuid "d39